      <h1>Second correction: Umbra's current development target</h1>
      <div class="blog-post-meta">
        <span class="blog-post-date">Originally August 12, 2026 · corrected August 28 and August 29, 2026</span>
        <span class="blog-post-tag">correction</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="blog-article-lede">The August 28 correction is now stale. Umbra's active development tree moved from the stated DGX Spark/CUDA target to Apple Silicon and MLX. A working private prototype exists, but there is no public build, supported hardware matrix, benchmark set, support commitment, or release date.</p>

    <div class="blog-body">
      <h2>What is current</h2>
      <p>Umbra's private development tree includes a browser interface, a Tauri desktop shell, a local FastAPI backend, and MLX inference on Apple Silicon. It is a working development prototype, not a public or supported release.</p>

      <h2>What has been removed</h2>
      <p>The original post repeated the now-withdrawn claim that a 397B mixture-of-experts model had been run on a 32&nbsp;GB MacBook through custom Metal work. It also stated projected resident-model, activation-capture, and multi-model behavior as though those capabilities had already been measured. Those statements should not be relied on without exact artifacts, commands, raw logs, and repeatable measurements.</p>

      <h2>Current status</h2>
      <p>The <a href="/umbra/">Umbra page</a> records the current private prototype without making performance or release claims. Any future performance claim should name the hardware, model revision, quantization, runtime, command, workload, measurement method, and raw result.</p>

